00:00.500 --> 00:03.300 align:start position:10%
<v.curator Dr. Halvorsen>Welcome, everyone, to the textile conservation studio here at the

00:03.300 --> 00:06.500
<v Dr. Halvorsen>Ashfield Museum. The garment on the table behind me is

00:06.500 --> 00:09.500
<v Dr. Halvorsen>the reason we are all whispering.

00:09.500 --> 00:13.000
<v Dr. Halvorsen>It is a <i>court&nbsp;mantua</i> of <i>Spitalfields&nbsp;silk</i>,

00:13.000 --> 00:15.600 align:middle
<v Dr. Halvorsen>woven around 1740, and it is the single most fragile

00:15.600 --> 00:18.400
<v Dr. Halvorsen>object in our entire collection.

00:23.400 --> 00:26.600
<v Dr. Halvorsen>The first enemy of a silk this old is not

00:26.600 --> 00:29.600
<v Dr. Halvorsen>moths, it is the weather.

00:29.600 --> 00:33.100 line:85%
<v Dr. Halvorsen>We hold this room at a steady 50&#37; relative humidity

00:33.100 --> 00:35.700
<v Dr. Halvorsen>all year, because silk that dries out grows brittle and

00:35.700 --> 00:38.500
<v Dr. Halvorsen>cracks, while silk kept too damp invites mould.

00:43.500 --> 00:46.700
<v Dr. Halvorsen>The second enemy is light.

00:46.700 --> 00:49.700 align:start position:10%
<v Dr. Halvorsen>Light fades the dyes and,

00:49.700 --> 00:53.200
<v Dr. Halvorsen>worse, it slowly rots the silk fibres themselves.

00:53.200 --> 00:55.800
<v Dr. Halvorsen>That is why the gallery is kept so dim.

00:55.800 --> 00:58.600
<v Dr. Halvorsen>We cap the light falling on this mantua at just

00:58.600 --> 01:01.800 align:middle
<v Dr. Halvorsen><b>50&nbsp;lux</b>, roughly the brightness of candlelight,

01:01.800 --> 01:04.800
<v Dr. Halvorsen>and we rotate it off display for long rests in

01:04.800 --> 01:08.300
<v Dr. Halvorsen>the dark.

01:13.300 --> 01:15.900
<v Dr. Halvorsen>When a garment like this arrives with an insect infestation,

01:15.900 --> 01:18.700 line:85%
<v Dr. Halvorsen>we never reach for a spray.

01:18.700 --> 01:21.900
<v Dr. Halvorsen>Instead we seal it in a sealed chamber and flush

01:21.900 --> 01:24.900
<v Dr. Halvorsen>all the oxygen out with <b>nitrogen&nbsp;gas</b>.

01:24.900 --> 01:28.400
<v Dr. Halvorsen>That anoxic treatment suffocates every egg and larva without putting

01:28.400 --> 01:31.000 align:start position:10%
<v Dr. Halvorsen>a single harsh chemical near the historic fibres.

01:36.000 --> 01:38.800
<v Dr. Halvorsen>Now, the actual mending.

01:38.800 --> 01:42.000
<v Dr. Halvorsen>Where the silk has split,

01:42.000 --> 01:45.000
<v Dr. Halvorsen>we support it from behind with a whisper thin sheer

01:45.000 --> 01:48.500 align:middle
<v Dr. Halvorsen>fabric called <lang fr>cr&#xEA;peline</lang>,

01:48.500 --> 01:51.100
<v Dr. Halvorsen>dyed to match, and we anchor the fragile threads down

01:51.100 --> 01:53.900
<v Dr. Halvorsen>with rows of tiny <i>couching&nbsp;stitches</i> in a fine silk thread

01:53.900 --> 01:57.100
<v Dr. Halvorsen>you can barely see.

02:02.100 --> 02:05.100 line:85%
<v Dr. Halvorsen>For the most powdery,

02:05.100 --> 02:08.600
<v Dr. Halvorsen>shattered areas, stitching alone is too risky,

02:08.600 --> 02:11.200
<v Dr. Halvorsen>so we turn to a heat activated conservation adhesive,

02:11.200 --> 02:14.000
<v Dr. Halvorsen>coated onto a fine net and tacked down with a

02:14.000 --> 02:17.200 align:start position:10%
<v Dr. Halvorsen>warm spatula. Every material we introduce is chosen to be

02:17.200 --> 02:20.200
<v Dr. Halvorsen>reversible, so a future conservator can undo our work without

02:20.200 --> 02:23.700
<v Dr. Halvorsen>harming the original.

02:28.700 --> 02:31.300
<v Dr. Halvorsen>Everything this mantua touches in storage is <i>acid&nbsp;free</i>.

02:31.300 --> 02:34.100 align:middle
<v Dr. Halvorsen>It rests on <i>acid&nbsp;free</i> tissue,

02:34.100 --> 02:37.300
<v Dr. Halvorsen>padded into gentle rolls rather than folded,

02:37.300 --> 02:40.300
<v Dr. Halvorsen>because a hard crease in silk this brittle would eventually

02:40.300 --> 02:43.800
<v Dr. Halvorsen>snap the fibres clean through.

02:48.800 --> 02:51.400 line:85%
<v Dr. Halvorsen>Before we touch a single thread,

02:51.400 --> 02:54.200
<v Dr. Halvorsen>though, we study the object.

02:54.200 --> 02:57.400
<v Dr. Halvorsen>We identify the original dyes under the microscope,

02:57.400 --> 03:00.400
<v Dr. Halvorsen>we map every earlier repair,

03:00.400 --> 03:03.900 align:start position:10%
<v Dr. Halvorsen>and only then do we decide how little we can

03:03.900 --> 03:06.500
<v Dr. Halvorsen>safely do. In conservation the guiding rule is always the

03:06.500 --> 03:09.300
<v Dr. Halvorsen>same: do the minimum,

03:09.300 --> 03:12.500
<v Dr. Halvorsen>and make it reversible.
